    <!-- 09 Hook Testing -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="testing">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">09 &mdash; Testing</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Hook testing: the highest-leverage test in advertising</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Same body + same CTA + 5 different hooks = isolated hook test</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">OKAD tests 5&ndash;10 hook variants per concept with $50&ndash;$100/day each</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Hook tests produce actionable data within 3 days</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Hook testing is the foundation of the <a href="/guide" class="text-accent hover:underline">creative testing</a> hierarchy. Because hooks have the highest impact on performance, testing hooks first produces the fastest and most reliable learnings.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">How to run a hook test</h3>
        <ol class="list-none p-0 counter-reset-step space-y-0">
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Keep the body and CTA identical.</strong> The only variable is the first 3 seconds. This isolates the hook's impact.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Produce 5&ndash;10 hook variants.</strong> Use different hook types: question, stat, before/after, controversy, "I tried X." Cast with different creators if possible.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Launch all variants simultaneously.</strong> Same audience, same placement, same budget ($50&ndash;$100/day per variant).</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Read hook rates after 24&ndash;48 hours.</strong> At 1,000+ impressions per variant, hook rate is directionally reliable.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Decide after 3&ndash;5 days.</strong> Kill hooks below 20%. Iterate hooks in the 20&ndash;30% range. Scale hooks above 30%. Then test body narratives against the winning hooks.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Hook rate and CPA correlation</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Hook rate and CPA are correlated but not perfectly. A high hook rate means more people see the body and CTA, which generally leads to more conversions at a lower cost. But a hook that attracts the wrong audience (high hook rate from curiosity-seekers who never buy) can have a high hook rate and bad CPA. This is why hook rate is a diagnostic metric, not the decision metric. CPA is always the final judge.</p>

