          { q: "What is a good hook rate?", a: "On Meta in 2026: 30-40% is good, 35%+ is strong, below 20% indicates a problem. These benchmarks vary by category and platform — entertainment ads tend to score higher than B2B ads. Track your own account's historical performance alongside industry averages." },
          { q: "Is hook rate the same as thumb-stop ratio?", a: "Yes. Hook rate, thumb-stop ratio, and thumb-stop rate are different names for the same metric: 3-second video views divided by impressions. The terminology varies by team and platform, but they all measure whether the first 3 seconds stopped the scroll." },
          { q: "How do I find hook rate in Meta Ads Manager?", a: "It's not a default column. Go to Columns > Customize columns > Create Custom Metric. Set the formula to 3-Second Video Views / Impressions, format as percentage. Save it to your default reporting view." },
          { q: "My hook rate is good but CPA is bad. What's wrong?", a: "The hook is working — people are watching. The problem is in the body, offer, or CTA. People watch but don't convert. Keep the hook and test different body narratives, offers, or calls to action. This is the most common diagnostic use of hook rate." },
          { q: "My hook rate is bad. What should I change?", a: "Replace the hook entirely. Try: faster product reveal, specific numbers instead of vague claims, unexpected visuals, bold text overlays, movement in the first frame, and cutting the preamble. Produce 5-10 different hook types and test them against the same body." },
